We thank the reviewers for their constructive and educated comments. In particular, we very much appreciated their interest towards the visualization question addressed by our paper, and their suggestion to revise some paragraphs and to complete the experimental section, so as to clarify the added value of our approach compared to previous art.  

Based on their recommendations, the main revisions of our paper can be listed as follows:  

  1) ***Figure 1, Equation (3)*** and their associated descriptions, which present the key ingredients of our method, have been revised to address the clarification issues raised by some reviewers; 

  2) The ablation study has been completed (Appendix B). It now demonstrates the ***importance of merging*** the activation maps (see Fig. 6 and 7), ***and of*** integrating ***the LNorm operator*** in Equation (3) (see Fig. 13); 

  3) Our proposed method is now positioned and compared with respect to two very recent methods that had not been considered at submission time, namely ***Zoom-CAM*** (ICPR 2021) and ***Layer-CAM*** (IEEE Trans. Image Processing, June 2021). The visual comparison provided in Figure 3, and extended in Figure 5 (in Appendix A), is largely in favour of our approach; For a more extensive comparison, Zoom-CAM and Layer-CAM saliency maps have been added to the interactive Jupyter notebook provided in supplementary material to visualize the maps associated to the subset of 2000 images from ILSVRC 2012, for all methods considered in the paper.

  4) The ***insertion and deletion metrics*** are provided for CAM-based methods, but also ***for gradient- and perturbation-based methods*** (see Appendix E). Without surprise, the deletion metric is better for gradient-based methods since those methods specifically identify the image pixels whose alteration has the largest impact on the training loss, and thus the network prediction. However, gradient-based methods perform poorly in terms of insertion. This is not surprising when looking at the gradient-based saliency maps: they fail in locating the whole visual patterns required to activate a class (see the IntegratedGrad and SmoothGrad maps provided in Figure 3 and 5) In contrast, our Poly-CAM-based approach provides relevant maps, and appears to achieve the best trade-off between deletion and insertion metrics since it optimizes the insertion-deletion metric; 

  5) A ***sensitivity analysis*** has been conducted (see Section F in Supplementary). Interestingly, this analysis reveals that, despite its accurate localization capability, our Poly-CAM method still inherits the small sensitivity that characterizes CAM-based approaches compared to gradient- and perturbation-based approaches. 

As a whole, these revisions, and especially the new experiments, add much value to our paper, and we thank the reviewers for highlighting their relevance.  

We also put a lot of effort to account for the comments and requests for clarification that were specific to each individual reviewer, as described in our reviewer-specific replies.
